  "id": "innu_teueikan_nikamu",
  "name": "Innu nikamu (teueikan drum songs)",
  "family": "global",
  "lineage": "Drum songs of the Innu of Nitassinan in Quebec and Labrador, sung by a man accompanying himself on the teueikan, a large hide frame drum with snares strung across its heads that add a buzzing rattle. The songs are received in dreams and tied to the caribou hunt, and are sung in thanks and at the makushan, the feast of caribou bone marrow, where people dance. The Canadian Museum of History and Labrador Innu community projects hold field recordings. The hand-drum card stands in for the teueikan.",
  "recipe": "classic modern innu nikamu, hunting Song, indigenous branch. solo with instrumental accompaniment. hand drum source unspecified. field recording. ORTF stereo pair, portable field-recorder pre. 24-bit digital.",
